A||N||11||007DOUG||What to do when your throttle cable breaks||||6||
Z||000000||007DOUG||06-26-2001||08:46 AM||||Don't know if this belongs in tech or chat, but here it goes......<P>So, here I am, 50 miles from home and the throttle cable breaks. Think Doug, think. (5 years of college tends to dull your mind). Ah-ha, use the cruise cable for throttle. Its just long enough to reach the driver's door:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/whattodo_2.jpg"> <P>Oh yeah, its funny. But it got me home. Just imagine how hard it was driving a STICK shift and trying to synchronize my new hand throttle with the clutch. Yep, there was a lot of my right foot stabbing at a dead pedal. Ummm....stalled a few times too.<P>Thankfully it wasn't raining.<P><P>------------------<BR>4 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>2 86 Red GTs (twins) <BR>86 GT Yellow ASAP<BR>86 GT Blue w/4:10 tranny<BR>86 GT Pearl White 3.4 T-top<BR>88 Faded red Formula 5 speed<BR>2 Black 88 GT's (twins)<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||6
Z||000001||84Bill||06-26-2001||08:52 AM||||<A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sounds/LOL.wav" TARGET=_blank>LOL!!</A>  That must have been fun. <P>I was wondering why didn't you use the resume/accel switch of the cruise control?<BR>Good to see you made it home thow ||||reg||14
Z||000002||JetroGT||06-26-2001||08:53 AM||||My old 86 2M4 had a melted throttle cable. The plastic wrapping came undone, "melted" and when I would step on the gas it would compress the sleve insted of pulling on the throttle! I used electrical tape and a file to make a splint to get the thing home. <BR>BTW, that cable is a pain to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||06-26-2001||08:54 AM||||Wow!<P>Hehe... at least it got you home and that's all that matters.  I bet you got some weird looks from people.||||reg||1
Z||000004||beachbomb||06-26-2001||06:03 PM||||Hey doug, i'll take that car off your hands for ya!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. I replaced my trottle cable shortly after buying my Indy( i agreee, i was a pretty good pain in the a** to replace, but glad i did it!)...take car..Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||6
Z||000005||KRMFiero||06-26-2001||06:15 PM||||I replaced the Trottle cable in my 85 duke, it was actually pretty easy, as long as you can bend easy and have about 1-2 hours (you have to take out your hole center section basically  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Huck||06-26-2001||06:21 PM||||Heh.. thats a completely different method I used when my throttle cable broke in my 86 2m4 .....  I was about 20km from home when it blew on a side road at a bottom of a hill (I had to take a leak REAL bad)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ..  I just ripped the spring off the throttle on the engine, had some wire in my trunk, and manually set my idle at about 2000 rpm...  then I just drove home... careful with the gears... I actually got her up to about 85km/h (thats about 55mph)<P>worked great, and my girlfriend thought I was a genius!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Huck<BR>87 GT T-top||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||06-26-2001||08:21 PM||||Doug, I know you West Virginia types are good at coming up with "cheap" ways to fix things (and then liking them so much they become a permanent fixture), but this is a little excessive, don't ya think?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>btw, the SE passed inspection saturday!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||06-26-2001||10:20 PM||||That's great!  I admire people who can get by on sheer genius!  If they'd have put that on the old "MacGyver" show(sp?), nobody would've believed it...<P>Nice blue GT, also - what I can see of it!<p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-27-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000009||Ed||06-27-2001||01:37 AM||||Many years ago I was driving a carbed pick up truck when the throttle cable broke. <P>I used a boot lace to the carb.<P>We also had a '55 Chevy pickup truck with a hand  throttle.  You could pull that out as a sort of primitive cruise control.  Illegal, dangerous, but it worked.||||reg||1
Z||000010||AusFiero||06-27-2001||08:58 AM||||lol... I had to do similar in a mini I had years ago the cable broke so I tied my shoelaces together and attatched that to a piece of the remaining cable that I pulled out uned the edge of the bonnet. Got me home. Can relate to the sychronisation bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mark||06-27-2001||07:27 PM||||Seems like averyone has had this pleasure at one time or another.  I once used the power cord from my 12 volt trouble light as a makeshift throttle cable.  I knew a guy whose fuel pump failed in the middle of the Idaho dessert... He used his windshield washer bottle to gravity-feed gas to the carburetor in his Honda Accord (he held it high outside the drivers window as hed drove! ||||reg||1
